/*******************************************************************
 * File:     ro5_bridge
 * Purpose:  The UART backend of Martin's BluetoothVoice host bridge
 *           (the btv_ro5_* interface of port/h/uart_bridge) for the
 *           built-in radio of a Raspberry Pi 4 under 32-bit RISC OS 5.
 *           Compiled in place of his port/c/uart_bridge (which drives the
 *           Pinebook Pro's radio); nothing else in his tree changes.
 *
 * The same job the Phoenix kernel's drivers/bt/bt_uart.c does at boot,
 * redone as a polled module backend:
 *
 *   inspect  RISC OS 5 on a Pi (BCMVideo is there); reads no hardware
 *   open     maps GPIO, UART0 and the VideoCore mailbox; checks the
 *            board is a Pi 4 / Pi 400 / CM4; checks UART0 is not the
 *            serial port; pins 30-33 to UART0 (ALT3); PL011 at 115200,
 *            8N1, FIFOs, hardware RTS/CTS
 *   boot     a step at a time: BT_ON off and on (firmware GPIO expander
 *            pin 0, through the mailbox), HCI Reset, Download Minidriver,
 *            the .hcd patch record by record (btm_firmware_load), the
 *            patch's own launch, Reset again, Read Local Version, Read
 *            BD_ADDR
 *   submit   the H4 type byte and the packet into the PL011 FIFO
 *   poll     the PL011 FIFO into a staging ring, through Martin's H4
 *            framer (btuartcalc), into an event queue and an ACL queue
 *   cancel, close (UART off, BT_ON low), counts, stats
 *
 * Tickets, as in Phoenix's phx_bridge: the top 8 bits hold the transfer
 * kind and the low 24 a sequence number.  EVENT_IN / ACL_IN tickets claim
 * "the next packet of that kind"; COMMAND / ACL_OUT are written to the
 * FIFO before submit returns, so their first poll answers OK.
 *
 * Flow control.  When the queues are full the framer stops, the staging
 * ring fills, this side stops reading the PL011, its receive FIFO fills
 * and the PL011 drops RTS (RTSEn): the controller holds its data.
 *
 * Written in C90 with no inline assembler so that both GCCSDK and the
 * Norcroft compiler take it.  Device pages from OS_Memory 13 are mapped
 * uncached and unbuffered, so no barriers are needed between accesses.
 *
 * Hardware facts (BCM2711, ARM physical, "low peripheral" mode):
 *   GPIO   0xFE200000  GPFSEL3 (+0x0C) holds pins 30-39, 3 bits each
 *   UART0  0xFE201000  PL011, 48 MHz reference clock (firmware default)
 *   MBOX   0xFE00B880  read +0x00, status +0x18, write +0x20; channel 8
 *   BT_ON  firmware GPIO expander pin 0 = GPIO 128 (mailbox tags)
 *   pins   GPIO30 CTS, 31 RTS, 32 TXD, 33 RXD, all UART0 on ALT3
 *******************************************************************/
